  <section>
    <div class="container-wide">
      <div class="section-label">Why the campaign worked</div>
      <h2>Six decisions made the outreach credible and commercially useful.</h2>
      <div class="cs2-six">
        <article class="cs2-card"><span class="cs2-kicker">Market</span><h3>Lending before FinTech</h3><p>The campaign owned a specific workflow and buyer group instead of treating every financial company as equivalent.</p></article>
        <article class="cs2-card"><span class="cs2-kicker">Language</span><h3>Process before promise</h3><p>The call explored evidence handling before making claims about automation or improvement.</p></article>
        <article class="cs2-card"><span class="cs2-kicker">Signals</span><h3>Question before conclusion</h3><p>Observable events shaped research but never replaced buyer confirmation.</p></article>
        <article class="cs2-card"><span class="cs2-kicker">Execution</span><h3>Human judgment</h3><p>Callers could listen, find the right owner, protect nuance, and disqualify weak interest.</p></article>
        <article class="cs2-card"><span class="cs2-kicker">Qualification</span><h3>Workflow before calendar</h3><p>Each meeting required fit, ownership, process context, a reason, and a defined purpose.</p></article>
        <article class="cs2-card"><span class="cs2-kicker">Handoff</span><h3>Context before demo</h3><p>The product team received the buyer's situation and questions instead of an empty invitation.</p></article>
      </div>
      <div class="cs2-confidentiality"><strong>Client confidentiality:</strong> The company name and identifying details are withheld. Campaign figures are presented within the stated scope and period.</div>
    </div>
  </section>

